"""Additional claims from the completely read Codex161-6 transcriptions.
Codex notes are leads, so these create no Stage1 indexed-entry verdicts.
"""
from build_stage2 import *

c([315],['68'],[44],[PRO+':418'],'Extension to symmetric, tensor, exterior algebras.','A module automorphism acts functorially on its tensor, symmetric and exterior algebras; an infinitesimal endomorphism extends by the derivation rule. Uniform nilpotence on the entire unbounded tensor algebra need not survive.','stated without proof','On tensor degree d the generator is the sum over d commuting tensor positions. Each fixed degree has a finite nilpotence bound if u is nilpotent; across all degrees only local nilpotence is guaranteed. Relations defining symmetric/exterior quotients are preserved.','B','Standard representation tensor constructions apply; the source phrase is an extension lead, not a uniform-nilpotence theorem.',['Milne2017'])
c([],['161-6'],[4],[G1+':11–33'],'SO(E) simply transitive on unit orthogonal pairs; O(E) doublement transitive.','In oriented Euclidean dimension3, an ordered orthonormal pair extends to exactly one positively oriented orthonormal frame, giving a simply transitive SO3 action. O3 is transitive with a stabilizer of order2.','stated without proof','Map the completed oriented frames to one another. In O3 the normal vector can acquire either sign. If doublement transitive means2-transitive in modern permutation terminology, it is false: pairwise metric invariants obstruct that reading.','B','Established orthogonal-frame geometry; SimõesPereira2016 uses the same rotational setting. The historical wording is not silently modernized.',['SimoesPereira2016'])
c([],['161-6'],[4,5,18],[G1+':34–110',G2+':2–94'],'Squared distances2(1−cosα),4sin²α sin²(θ/2), and equal-triangle relation.','For unit u and orthonormal tangent pair v,w, x_i=u cosα+(v cos(iθ)+w sin(iθ))sinα. Distances from u are2(1−cosα); consecutive distances are4sin²α sin²(θ/2). Equality implies cosα=cosθ/(1−cosθ).','proof sketched','Expand dot products. For a nonzero triangle divide by sin²(α/2), obtaining cos(α/2)=1/(2sin(θ/2)) in the chosen positive range. With0<θ<π, a nonzero positively oriented triple requiresθ>π/3; equalityθ=π/3 is the collapsed case. θ=π gives dependent triples.','B','Euclidean equal-edge geometry, independently checked by exact identities; later rotational literature confirms the resulting icosahedral realization.',['SimoesPereira2016'],calc=True)
c([],['161-6'],[5,18],[G1+':86–110',G2+':66–94'],'θ=2π/5 gives cosα=1/√5 and12 points; circle centre u radius4sin²(α/2).','The12 points ±u,±x_i are distinct regular-icosahedral vertices. The latitude circle has centre cosα·u and radius sinα;4sin²(α/2) is the squared chord distance from u.','false as stated','The angle and12-point construction are correct. The literal circle-centre/radius description mixes three different quantities and is false in Euclidean3-space. Exact coordinate calculations verify cosα=1/√5 and cos2α=−3/5.','C','The corrected classical realization is established; no source supports the literal centre/radius sentence.',['SimoesPereira2016'],calc=True)
c([],['161-6'],[5,6],[G1+':104–124'],'Cap closure, edge lengths, cap stabilizer dihedral10 and rotation stabilizer cyclic5.','A nondegenerate fivefold cap has squared lengths2∓2/√5; its full orthogonal stabilizer fixing the summit is D10 and its rotational stabilizer is C5. Distinct successive equal-edge points exhaust the pentagon and force closure.','proof sketched','Restriction to the latitude plane identifies the stabilizer with the regular pentagon symmetry group. Equal-edge adjacency gives the cycle C5; four distinct successive choices determine the fifth closure. The neighbouring-cap lemma must say summit x0, not u.','B','Classical finite rotational geometry; the exact counts and squared-length quadratic are independently verified.',['SimoesPereira2016'],calc=True)
c([],['161-6'],[6,7],[G1+':121–172'],'Neighbouring cap lemma; three identities à prouver; γx1 calculation unfinished.','The written rotation γ=R_w(α)R_u(−θ/2) sends u,x0,x1,x2,x3,x4 respectively to x0,−x2,−x3,x1,u,x4. Thus it carries the cap at u to the neighbouring cap at x0.','open on the pages','The script substitutes exact radicals, verifies γᵀγ=1,detγ=1 and all six images. This discharges the omitted last vector and the unfinished γx1 computation. The source’s prime labels represent antipodes; the erroneous summit wording is kept in the readings index.','B','The rotation statement is an exact classical computation, confirmed here rather than inferred from a broad paper.',['SimoesPereira2016'],calc=True)
c([],['161-6'],[8,9],[G1+':173–230'],'30 edges,20 triangular faces; minimum distance and all three pairwise neighbours deferred until transitivity.','The minimum-distance graph on the12 vertices has30 edges,20 triangles, degree5, and exactly the icosahedron face incidence.','proof sketched','Exact squared-distance enumeration gives30 minimum pairs and20 three-cliques. The rotation in the preceding claim and cap rotations prove transitivity; the local pentagon determines five incident triangles. The written edge/face lists are thereby checked, with the uncertain shifted face index left to ink.','B','Established icosahedral combinatorics. The finite enumeration independently verifies the deferred statements on these coordinates.',['SimoesPereira2016'],calc=True)
c([],['161-6'],[8,9],[G1+':173–230'],'Γ=μ2×Γ′; Γ′ acts freely transitively on oriented edges; Γ on ordered faces; orders60,120.','The full orthogonal symmetry group is the direct product of central inversion and its60-element rotation subgroup; rotations act simply transitively on60 directed edges and the full group on120 ordered triangular frames.','proof sketched','A directed edge fixes an oriented frame and hence a rotation; transitivity gives60. Ordered face vertices determine an orthogonal map; there are20·6=120. Central inversion is orientation reversing in dimension3 and central, giving the direct product. The exact automorphism enumeration yields60 matrices of each determinant.','B','SimoesPereira2016 identifies the60 rotations with A5; full versus rotational symmetry is explicitly separated in this run.',['SimoesPereira2016'],calc=True)
c([],['161-6'],[9,10],[G1+':232–239'],'Every automorphism of the icosahedral graph lies in Γ′; dual graph Γ|Γ′.','The full abstract automorphism group of the icosahedron graph, and of its dual dodecahedron graph, is Γ of order120, not Γ′ of order60.','false as stated','Central inversion preserves all minimum distances and is a graph automorphism, with determinant−1. A graph automorphism fixes an ordered face only if it fixes successive adjacent faces and all vertices; hence at most120. The120 orthogonal maps attain this bound. Dual incidence transports the same result.','C','The rotational A5 result does not imply the false full-automorphism assertion. Exact enumeration confirms the necessary factor2.',['SimoesPereira2016'],calc=True)
c([],['161-6'],[9,11],[G1+':216–240'],'Coherent cyclic orders at vertices; tangent-frame quotient by C5 and its fifth power.','The two orientations of the sphere induce two coherent cyclic orders on the five neighbours of every vertex. Γ′ preserves each, and orientation-reversing symmetries exchange them. Over the unit sphere S, the bundle of tangent frames modulo C5 is the fifth associated power of the unit tangent circle bundle.','stated without proof','A sphere orientation orders tangent directions. On each trivializing patch of the unit tangent bundle, quotient the S1 fibre by C5 and identify S1/C5 with S1 by [z]↦z5. Its transition functions become the fifth powers of the original functions, proving the precise bundle statement over S already named in the margin. The trailing soit is unfinished but does not erase that statement.','B','SageRibbonGraphs supplies the rotation-system formalism; the circle-bundle quotient is independently re-proved by transition functions. No unspecified moduli-space claim is inferred from the unfinished margin.',['SageRibbonGraphs','SimoesPereira2016'])
c([],['161-6'],[13,15],[G1+':242–323;393–498'],'ζ+ζ−1 satisfies t²+t−1; root exchange t↦t²−2; square(2t+1)=5.','In Z[1/5,T]/Φ5(T), t=T+T−1 satisfies t²+t−1=0. Its conjugate is−1−t=t²−2; trace and norm are both−1, discriminant5.','proof sketched','Reduce powers moduloΦ5; all listed identities hold exactly. The script computes inversion, the quadratic relation, trace/norm and discriminant. The unfinished norm is completed as our calculation.','B','Cyclotomic quadratic algebra is established; Ji2023 and Esquisse §4 use the same arithmetic-of-angles setting. No assertion of first discovery.',['Ji2023','Esquisse1997'],calc=True)
c([],['161-6'],[15],[G1+':393–418'],'Primitive fifth roots form a rank4 torsor under(Z/5)×.','Over Spec Z[1/5], Spec Z[1/5,T]/Φ5 is finite étale of rank4 and a torsor under C4=(Z/5)× acting by powers.','stated without proof','The roots of T5−1 are simple when5 is invertible; removing1 leaves four primitive roots. Over an algebraic closure, the power action is simply transitive, so the finite étale cover is a torsor.','B','Stacks03PK Lemma59.28.1 gives the precise invertibility/étaleness mechanism.',['Stacks03PK'])
c([],['161-6'],[15],[G1+':441–498'],'Which coefficients are fixed by inversion? c1=0,c2=c3.','For any Z[1/5]-algebra A, the inversion-fixed subalgebra of A[T]/Φ5 is A[t]/(t²+t−1), including characteristic2.','proved there','The constant coefficient of β(f)−f is−c1, so c1=0 without dividing by2; remaining equations give c2=c3. The fixed module is generated by1,T²+T³, equivalently1,t. Multiplication gives the quadratic relation. One intermediate sign is inconsistent but the final equations are correct.','B','Exact invariant-ring computation, verified over the universal integral quotient rather than only a field.',['Stacks03PK','Ji2023'],calc=True)
c([],['161-6'],[15],[G1+':433–440'],'A twist of μ_n is étale rank n over Z.','An ordinary faithfully flat form of μ_n is not étale at primes dividing n. The sheet’s symbol T and meaning of twisted group are not sufficiently defined to identify an alternative construction.','false as stated','For n=p in characteristic p, μ_p has coordinate algebra k[t]/(t−1)^p and is nonreduced, hence not étale. Étaleness descends and is invariant under a torsor twist. If the intended object is a twist of the constant group Z/n instead, that is different data.','F','Stacks03PK distinguishes the étale Kummer statement when n is invertible from the general fppf statement. The undefined T blocks a stronger identification.',['Stacks03PK'])
c([],['161-6'],[13,14,16],[G1+':324–392;499–560'],'Frame coproduct by rectangle generators; arbitrary-supremum inequality; finite-support generalization.','A frame K generated by finite meets of factor images has the expected coproduct universal map if the displayed separation condition d and a sufficient cover-refinement relation e hold. The transcription leaves e incomplete.','proof sketched','The join-generated condition gives uniqueness. The proposed supremum formula gives finite-meet preservation and restricts correctly using d. Preservation of all joins uses the strong rectangular grid-refinement assertion at14. It is an assumption needing verification, not automatic for arbitrary open covers. Scan14 is wholly cancelled; on16 only the upper proof block is cancelled, the finite-meet part remains live.','C','Products of locales/topoi have established site constructions; Lafforgue2024 slides47–48 supplies a correct product-topos comparison and warns through its local-compactness hypothesis against naive space products. This does not identify the missing axiom e.',['Lafforgue2024'],'medium')
c([],['161-6'],[17],[G1+':631–636'],'Hom_G(G/H,E)=E^H.','Evaluation at the identity coset gives a natural bijection between equivariant maps G/H→E and H-fixed points of E.','stated without proof','An equivariant map is determined by f(H), which must be H-fixed. Conversely x∈E^H defines f(gH)=gx, well-defined precisely by H-invariance.','B','Basic homogeneous-set equivalence underlies the later coset graph constructions. It is independently proved; the searched group/action literature treats it as elementary background.',['Milne2017'])
c([],['161-6'],[19],[G2+':95–174'],'Order60 group acting on15 points with commuting order3 permutation; assuming faithfulness on quotient5 gives A5.','A transitive action and a commuting permutation of order3 imply all its cycles have length3, so the quotient has5 points. If the induced G-action on that quotient is faithful, G is the index2 subgroup A5 of S5.','proof sketched','A fixed point of the commuting permutation would force it to fix every point by transitivity, contradicting order3. Faithfulness gives an injection of a60-element group into S5, whose unique index2 subgroup is A5. Without faithfulness take C60 acting through regular C15, then quotient C5. The ambiguous normalizer margin does not supply a complete proof of faithfulness.','B','SimoesPereira2016 gives a full rotational A5 identification; this conditional group-theoretic route is independently checked.',['SimoesPereira2016'])
c([],['161-6'],[18,19,20],[G2+':36–84;143–152;196–200;254–265'],'π comparisons; cosα=1−2sin²θ; α=60?','For θ=2π/5, α=arccos(1/√5)≈63.43495 degrees. Several exploratory lines using θ orθ/2 omit terms or have impossible inequalities; the60-degree trial is false.','open on the pages','The correct identity is cosα=1/(2sin²(θ/2))−1. Exact radicals show√3 sin(π/5)≠1; numerical residual≈0.0180739. The inequality4π/5<2π/3 is false. No repaired single derivation is imposed on the conflicting exploratory lines.','C','Classical trigonometric coordinates settle the numerical trial; the original paper’s scratch computations remain distinct.',['SimoesPereira2016'],calc=True)
c([],['161-6'],[20],[G2+':213–218;269–275'],'Coset right-translation and normalizer intersections.','Right multiplication gH↦gaH on G/H is well-defined iff a−1Ha⊂H; for an automorphism require equality. The displayed normalizer-intersection equality is not true for arbitrary subgroups.','false as stated','For the map, compare representatives gh. In S3 let H=< (12) >,H′=< (23) >. Their intersection is1, whose normalizer is S3, while N(H)∩N(H′)=1. The page may intend special icosahedral subgroups, but their uncertain labels do not establish those extra hypotheses.','F','The coset criterion is elementary and settled; the unidentified normalizer calculation cannot be assigned a stronger precise fate.',['SimoesPereira2016'])
c([],['161-6'],[22],[G2+':278–316'],'For c²+c−1=0, étale-local icosahedra exist; locally SO(Q)-conjugate; finite étale A5 stabilizers.','An arithmetic moduli claim about icosahedra with angle parameter c, requiring a definition of the configuration and restrictions on the quadratic form and base, including the roles of2 and5.','stated without proof','The sheet does not supply these hypotheses; its diagram suggests Z[1/4] while later expressions divide by5. Over characteristic0 the classical realization has rotational A5. Good-prime specializations of explicit matrices retain A5, but that does not prove étale-local existence/conjugacy over every scheme or handle the bad primes.','C','Esquisse1997 §4 pp21–25 presents the broader arithmetic program. Ji2023 §3.1 Proposition3.1 checks good-prime matrix specializations and explicitly excludes2,5 for his icosahedron formulas; it does not settle the exact scheme-level statement here.',['Esquisse1997','Ji2023'],'medium')
c([],['161-6'],[22],[G2+':326–371'],'r/(2−r)=(2r+1)/5; cos2α=−3/5; B²=(4/5)QQ and (B/2)²=(1/5)QQ.','For r²+r−1=0 with5 inverted, r/(2−r)=(2r+1)/5 and its square is1/5. If B is twice the Euclidean bilinear pairing, the two polar identities have the stated factors.','proof sketched','Exact quotient-ring reduction proves the rational identities. The half-polar normalization requires2 invertible. In characteristic2, B²=0 implies B=0 on field-valued points, but over a nonreduced base it need not; it does not by itself imply vector proportionality in arbitrary dimension or for degenerate Q.','C','Ji2023 explains that normalization changes which primes his formulas allow. The arithmetic identities here are verified, while the extrapolated characteristic2 geometric sentence needs more hypotheses.',['Ji2023','Esquisse1997'],calc=True)
c([],['161-6'],[23],[G2+':374–418'],'π(C,E)=Fun(Cop,E) is a topos; verify π(f,g)_*.','For small C and a Grothendieck topos E, Fun(Cop,E) is a Grothendieck topos. The inverse image for(f:C→C′,g:E→E′) is g^* pointwise after precomposition by fop; its right adjoint is Ran_fop followed pointwise by the direct image g_*.','open on the pages','Correct typed direct image: π(f,g)_*=g_*^{C′}∘Ran_fop^E, equivalently Ran_fop^{E′}∘g_*^C since g_* preserves limits. Verify the adjunction in two steps. A site D for E presents the functor category as sheaves on C×D with only D-covering conditions. The entire sheet is cancelled, including the displayed questions.','B','Lafforgue2024 slide47 gives the product-site theorem that supplies this presentation; the right-adjoint typing is independently checked.',['Lafforgue2024'])
c([],['161-6'],[23],[G2+':391–430'],'Is π(C,E)→Ĉ×TopE always an equivalence? groupoid and presheaf cases.','In the2-category of Grothendieck topoi and geometric morphisms, E^{Cop} is the product of E and Set^{Cop}; the two displayed projections are the canonical ones.','open on the pages','Present E=Sh(D,J). On C×D, trivial topology in C and J in D make a sheaf exactly a C-presheaf of J-sheaves, yielding the product by the product-site theorem. For groupoids this is the topos of internal constant-group actions; for E=Set^{Dop} it is Set^{(C×D)op}. Disjoint-union reductions follow componentwise. The entire passage is cancelled, not later refuted.','B','Exact general product-site result: Lafforgue2024 slide47, with trivial topology on C. Searches of SGA4 references did not locate an opened exact SGA locator, so none is fabricated.',['Lafforgue2024'])
c([],['161-6'],[25,26],[G2+':431–476'],'Th1: cyclic orders determine regular neighbourhood Uα and embedding isotopy classes.','A finite graph tamely embedded in an oriented surface has an oriented regular neighbourhood determined relative to the graph by the cyclic orders of incident darts; compatible embeddings extend over this neighbourhood and extensions are unique up to the relevant isotopy.','proof sketched','Glue one oriented disc per nonisolated vertex and one band per edge according to the cyclic orders; isolated vertices give discs, isolated circles give annuli. Local isotopy extension controls changes of chosen neighbourhoods. Number of cyclic orders at a valence d>0 vertex is(d−1)!.','B','SageRibbonGraphs implements the exact thickening data. Yagasaki2000 Theorem1.1 and Corollary1.1 supply extension/local-bundle results for compact subpolyhedra of PLsurfaces; ColinMesmay2014 §2.2 describes the resulting combinatorial map.',['SageRibbonGraphs','Yagasaki2000','ColinMesmay2014'])
c([],['161-6'],[26],[G2+':460–476'],'Number of collar components b1(X)+1 for connected X? b1+b0 generally?','For an oriented regular neighbourhood Uα of a finite graph, number of boundary components is b1(X)+b0(X)−2g(Uα), with genus summed over components.','open on the pages','Euler characteristic gives b0−b1=2b0−2g−b. A two-loop bouquet with alternating cyclic order thickens to a once-punctured torus: b1=2,b0=1,g=1,b=1, not3. The same graph with planar rotation has b=3.','C','The corrected ribbon-surface formula is established. SageRibbonGraphs gives explicit genus1,boundary1 examples, and the script independently enumerates boundary cycles.',['SageRibbonGraphs'],calc=True)
c([],['161-6'],[26,27],[G2+':476–533'],'Th2: classification by cyclic order α, partition of boundary set, genera and punctures of complements.','For a finite graph in an oriented finite-type surface, with every surface component meeting the graph, orientation-preserving homeomorphism classes relative to the labeled graph are determined by its ribbon neighbourhood and complementary components, their incident boundary subsets, genus and punctures.','proof sketched','Cut along the neighbourhood boundary and apply classification of orientable surfaces with labeled boundary components. Boundary orientation-preserving gluing maps have no extra homeomorphism-class invariant because circle maps can be absorbed by collars. This concerns homeomorphism classes; mapping classes/stabilizers enter only in the next sheets.','B','ColinMesmay2014 §2.2 and Lemma5 give the exact extended combinatorial-map criterion, including noncellular faces and surface boundary data.',['ColinMesmay2014'])
c([],['161-6'],[27],[G2+':514–533'],'Genus should be sum gλ+γ(α); verify.','For gluing oriented surface pieces along boundary circles, genus equals the sum of piece genera plus the first Betti number of the incidence graph. For connected U and final S, g(S)=g(U)+Σgλ+|J|−|Λ|.','open on the pages','Euler characteristic is additive across circle gluings. The connected bipartite incidence graph has |J| edges and1+|Λ| vertices, so its cycle rank is|J|−|Λ|. An annulus neighbourhood glued to one annulus complement along both boundaries gives a torus, although both pieces have genus0.','C','Standard surface Euler characteristic and extended map data settle the corrected formula; the conjectured sum misses the incidence cycles.',['SageRibbonGraphs','ColinMesmay2014'],calc=True)
c([],['161-6'],[28,29],[G2+':534–586'],'Restriction from oriented surface isomorphism classes is a transitive G-set, hence G/H.','Within one oriented-homeomorphism type of an embedding, the mapping class group acts transitively on embedding isotopy classes, with stabilizer H of the chosen graph embedding.','proof sketched','A chosen oriented isomorphism identifies the source torsor with G. Its equivariant surjection to embedding classes is then exactly the orbit map G→G/H. This is a set-level action; the topological quotient/fibration is a separate question.','B','ColinMesmay2014 supplies an exact description of when two orbit elements agree; Yagasaki2000 gives the corresponding local topological extension.',['ColinMesmay2014','Yagasaki2000'])
c([],['161-6'],[28,29],[G2+':555;587–591'],'Oriented mapping class group G≅Aut_ext π1(S).','For a closed oriented surface, the orientation-preserving mapping class group maps to orientation-preserving outer automorphisms, not in general to all Outπ1. Boundary and puncture versions need peripheral data and an explicit convention.','false as stated','On T², orientation-preserving mapping classes are SL2(Z), while Outπ1(T²)=GL2(Z). The determinant−1 automorphism cannot come from an orientation-preserving map. For higher genus the subgroup preserves the fundamental class. Epstein1966 Theorem6.3 verifies the homotopy-to-isotopy kernel statement used in the closed case.','C','Classical Dehn–Nielsen–Baer theory uses the orientation/peripheral restriction. Exact injectivity is matched to Epstein; the unrestricted displayed isomorphism is refuted by the torus.',['Epstein1966','FarbMargalit2011'])
c([],['161-6'],[29],[G2+':593–605'],'Surjective π1(X)→π1(S) implies H trivial; this means all complements discs? à vérifier.','For connected X and a closed oriented aspherical surface S, π1-surjectivity makes the embedding stabilizer H trivial. The proposed disc-complement characterization does not extend unchanged to punctured finite-type surfaces.','open on the pages','If hφ is isotopic toφ, their induced π1maps agree up to a common conjugation; surjectivity makes h_* inner, and mapping-class injectivity makes h isotopic toid. A core circle in the open annulus has π1-surjective inclusion, but its two complementary ends are punctured, not discs. The compact closed version can be proved by cutting/van Kampen.','C','Epstein1966 Theorem6.3 settles the compact kernel step. The source’s finite-type puncture ambiguity produces a real counterexample to its broader complement shorthand.',['Epstein1966','ColinMesmay2014'])
c([],['161-6'],[28,30],[G2+':550–556;612'],'If outer π1 maps agree on each component, is h in H?','For a finite graph X embedded in the interior of a compact oriented surface S and an orientation-preserving homeomorphism h, hφ is isotopic toφ iff for each component Xi the induced homomorphisms π1(Xi)→π1(S) agree up to inner conjugacy.','open on the pages','Equality of the outer maps gives a homotopy of each graph component, hence homotopy of the finite disjoint union. The existing h supplies the oriented-homeomorphism congruence. ColinMesmay2014 arXiv-version Theorem14 then gives a pointwise ambient isotopy; the converse follows from homotopy invariance. Sphere cases cause no obstruction since graph maps still have the stated homotopy classification.','B','Ladegaillerie1984 is the original published characterization as explicitly attributed in ColinMesmay2014; its arXiv-version Theorem14 re-proves it with a finite cycle test. Grothendieck’s Esquisse §3 pp13–14 reports the earlier1974–76 work and delayed1984 publication; no exact thesis circulation date is inferred.',['ColinMesmay2014','Ladegaillerie1984','Esquisse1997'])
c([],['161-6'],[27,29,30],[G2+':510–513;606–610'],'Make the boundary index set combinatorial; give a simple α criterion for genus0.','Let σ record cyclic dart order and τ reverse darts. Boundary components are the cycles of στ (up to the chosen composition convention); capping them gives genus0 precisely when v−e+number_cycles(στ)=2 for each connected nonempty component.','open on the pages','Trace one side of an edge: reverse the dart, then take the next dart around the vertex. This returns exactly the boundary traversal. Euler characteristic of the capped surface supplies the criterion. Isolated vertices and isolated circles are handled separately by disc/annulus neighbourhoods.','B','SageRibbonGraphs documents the permutation/thickening construction and computes boundary counts and genus. The script verifies both genus0 and genus1 rotations of a two-loop bouquet.',['SageRibbonGraphs'],calc=True)
c([],['161-6'],[31],[G2+':614–632'],'Topological G/H≅embedding space and long homotopy sequence??','For the restriction map from a surface homeomorphism group to the appropriate component of embeddings of a compact subpolyhedron, local extension gives a principal bundle with fibre the subgroup fixing X. Its homotopy long exact sequence has …π1H→π1G→π1Emb→π0H→π0G→π0Emb.','open on the pages','Yagasaki2000 Corollary1.1 supplies the needed principal-bundle hypothesis in the PLsurface/compact-polyhedron setting, rather than assuming every topological quotient is a fibration. The ambiguous π1|π2 overG must beπ1 in that displayed segment; π2Emb belongs one position earlier, beforeπ1H.','B','Yagasaki2000 Corollary1.1 exactly validates the restriction-bundle mechanism; Yagasaki2002 classifies many component homotopy types but is not needed to infer arbitrary π0orbit equality.',['Yagasaki2000','Yagasaki2002'])
c([],['161-6'],[33],[G2+':633–670'],'Graph automorphisms and isolated vertices/circles; permutation product.','A graph automorphism is a compatible pair of permutations of vertices and darts. For the special graph with isolated vertices and isolated circular edges, AutΓ=Perm(S)×(C2 wr Perm(A/τ)).','stated without proof','The general group embeds in Perm(S)×Perm(A) and is cut out by incidence and involution compatibility; it is not the unrestricted product. In the isolated case vertex and dart actions are independent, and a permutation commuting with a fixed-point-free involution permutes the two-element orbits and independently flips them.','B','The permutation-graph formalism is established; the exact wreath-product decomposition is independently checked.',['SageRibbonGraphs','Milne2017'])
c([],['161-6'],[33],[G2+':671–703'],'Cancelled transitive loop graph classification by G⊃H′⊃H and involution in normalizer quotient.','For transitive dart and vertex G-sets with all edges loops, choose A=G/H,S=G/H′,H⊂H′. Reversal is right multiplication by a nontrivial order2 coset in(H′∩N_G(H))/H; faithful action means core_G(H)=1.','proof sketched','Equivariant self-maps of G/H correspond to normalizer cosets. Fixed-point-freeness requires the coset nontrivial; preserving the vertex map requires its representative inH′. Since core(H)⊂core(H′), the kernel on the entire graph is core(H). The entire case b block is cancelled, although the mathematical classification is sound.','B','Elementary homogeneous-set classification, re-proved here; the source’s cancellation records drafting status, not mathematical falsity.',['Milne2017','SageRibbonGraphs'])
c([],['161-6'],[34],[G2+':704–727'],'For nonloop edges, prove injectivity iff H′∩σ−1H′σ⊂H.','With A=G/H,S=G/H′ and reversal representative σ∈N_G(H),σ²∈H,σ∉H, the pair-of-endpoints map gH↦(gH′,gσH′) is injective iff H′∩σH′σ−1=H (equivalently the written conjugate). Nonloops requireσ∉H′.','open on the pages','Two cosets have equal endpoints iff g′−1g lies in both H′ andσH′σ−1. Sinceσ²∈H⊂H′ the two conjugate forms agree. H is contained in the intersection, so the written inclusion is equality. The boxed stabilizer labels G_a,G_s are swapped relative to this correctly typed map; preserve them as a reading alarm.','B','The elementary coset criterion is settled by direct calculation, without supposing the group is finite.',['Milne2017','SageRibbonGraphs'])

